window.__labLoaded({"id":"4.5","title":"The Bootstrap (Bonus)","goal":"Bootstrap one small sample of five heart rates: get a standard error and a 95% interval without any formula, then compare with the t-interval.","question":"A researcher has only five resting heart rates (62, 70, 68, 75, 65 bpm). How uncertain is their mean, and can we answer without trusting a formula or the assumption of normal data? We will resample the five values thousands of times (the bootstrap) and compare the result with the classic t-interval.","python":{"blocks":[{"title":"The sample","text":"Five resting heart rates (bpm) from the lesson. The classic formula SE = s / sqrt(n) is our benchmark.","code":"import numpy as np\nfrom scipy import stats\n\ndata = np.array([62, 70, 68, 75, 65])\nn = len(data)\nse = data.std(ddof=1) / np.sqrt(n)\n\nprint(f'mean = {data.mean():.1f}')\nprint(f's    = {data.std(ddof=1):.2f}')\nprint(f'classic SE = {se:.2f}')","explain":["Load NumPy for arrays and arithmetic.","Load SciPy's stats tools, which we need for the t-interval.","Store the five heart rates in an array.","Count them: n = 5 is a very small sample.","Classic standard error: sample SD (ddof=1) divided by the square root of n.","Print the sample mean.","Print the sample SD.","Print the classic standard error, our benchmark."],"meaning":"The mean heart rate is 68.0 bpm, the SD is 4.95 and the classic standard error is 2.21. So the formula says the true average is known to within about 2 bpm. Next we get this uncertainty without the formula.","out":"mean = 68.0\ns    = 4.95\nclassic SE = 2.21","figs":[]},{"title":"Resample 5,000 times","text":"Each resample draws 5 values from our data with replacement (the same person can appear twice), then takes the mean. We keep all 5,000 means.","code":"rng = np.random.default_rng(2025)\nresamples = rng.choice(data, size=(5000, n), replace=True)\nboot_means = resamples.mean(axis=1)\n\nprint('one resample:', resamples[0], ' mean =', resamples[0].mean())\nprint(f'average of boot means = {boot_means.mean():.2f}')","explain":["Create a seeded random generator so results are repeatable.","Resample WITH replacement: each pretend sample can reuse the same real reading, which mimics drawing a fresh sample. Repeat 5,000 times.","Average each resample, giving 5,000 bootstrap means.","Print the first resample and its mean to see what a resample looks like.","Print the average of all bootstrap means; it should be close to the real mean."],"meaning":"One resample was 68, 65, 65, 70, 65: the value 65 appears three times and 62 and 75 are missing, which is normal for sampling with replacement. Its mean is 66.6. Across 5,000 resamples the average mean is 68.04, close to the real 68.0.","out":"one resample: [68 65 65 70 65]  mean = 66.6\naverage of boot means = 68.04","figs":[]},{"title":"Bootstrap SE and interval","text":"The SD of the bootstrap means is the bootstrap standard error. The 2.5th and 97.5th percentiles of the means form a 95% percentile interval.","code":"boot_se = boot_means.std(ddof=1)\nlow, high = np.percentile(boot_means, [2.5, 97.5])\n\nprint(f'bootstrap SE = {boot_se:.2f}')\nprint(f'95% percentile interval = ({low:.1f}, {high:.1f})')","explain":["The SD of the 5,000 bootstrap means is the bootstrap standard error: how much a mean of 5 readings wobbles.","Take the 2.5th and 97.5th percentiles of the bootstrap means: the middle 95% of them forms a 95% interval.","Print the bootstrap standard error.","Print the 95% percentile interval."],"meaning":"The bootstrap standard error is 1.99, close to the classic 2.21, so the two methods agree about how precise the mean is. The middle 95% of resampled means runs from 64.4 to 72.0 bpm.","out":"bootstrap SE = 1.99\n95% percentile interval = (64.4, 72.0)","figs":[]},{"title":"Compare with the t-interval","text":"With only five values the t-interval has to be wide to be honest. The bootstrap interval is narrower, because resampling cannot invent information a tiny sample does not contain.","code":"t_low, t_high = stats.t.interval(0.95, df=n - 1, loc=data.mean(), scale=se)\n\nprint(f't-interval        = ({t_low:.1f}, {t_high:.1f})')\nprint(f'bootstrap interval = ({low:.1f}, {high:.1f})')\nprint(f'widths: t = {t_high - t_low:.1f}, bootstrap = {high - low:.1f}')","explain":["Compute the classic 95% t-interval for comparison; df = n - 1 and scale is the standard error.","Print the t-interval.","Print the bootstrap interval from the last step.","Print both widths (upper minus lower) so we can compare them directly."],"meaning":"The t-interval is (61.9, 74.1), width 12.3, while the bootstrap interval is (64.4, 72.0), width 7.6. With only five values the t-interval is deliberately wide and honest. The bootstrap interval is narrower because resampling cannot add information; it tends to be too optimistic in tiny samples.","out":"t-interval        = (61.9, 74.1)\nbootstrap interval = (64.4, 72.0)\nwidths: t = 12.3, bootstrap = 7.6","figs":[]}]},"r":{"code":"hr <- c(62, 70, 68, 75, 65)\nset.seed(2025)\nboot_means <- replicate(5000, mean(sample(hr, replace = TRUE)))\nsd(boot_means)\nquantile(boot_means, c(0.025, 0.975))\nt.test(hr)$conf.int","expect":"bootstrap sd about 1.99; 2.5% and 97.5% quantiles about 64.4 and 72; t.test conf.int 61.85 to 74.15 (Python: 1.99, (64.4, 72.0), (61.9, 74.1))","note":"sample(hr, replace = TRUE) draws a resample of the same size. Bootstrap numbers are close to Python's but not identical; the t-interval is exact.","explain":["Store the five heart rates.","Fix the random seed.","Resample the data with replacement 5,000 times and keep the mean of each resample.","Print the SD of the bootstrap means (bootstrap SE).","Print the 2.5% and 97.5% quantiles, the percentile interval.","Print the classic t-interval for comparison."],"read":"sd(boot_means) should be about 1.99 and the quantiles about 64.4 and 72. The t.test line prints 61.85 and 74.15. Bootstrap numbers are close to Python's, not identical, but the t-interval is exact."},"spss":{"menu":"Analyze > Descriptive Statistics > Explore, then press the Bootstrap button and tick Perform bootstrapping (Number of samples 5000, Percentile)","syntax":"DATA LIST FREE / hr.\nBEGIN DATA\n62 70 68 75 65\nEND DATA.\nBOOTSTRAP\n  /SAMPLING METHOD=SIMPLE\n  /VARIABLES INPUT=hr\n  /CRITERIA CILEVEL=95 CITYPE=PERCENTILE NSAMPLES=5000\n  /MISSING USER=EXCLUDE.\nEXAMINE VARIABLES=hr\n  /PLOT=NONE\n  /STATISTICS=DESCRIPTIVES.","note":"The Bootstrap button appears on many dialogs. In the output, the Bootstrap table gives the bias, Std. Error and 95% percentile interval for the mean; the ordinary Descriptives table gives the t-interval. Results are close to Python's, not identical.","explain":["Declare one variable, hr, to read from the data lines.","Marks the start of the typed-in data.","The five heart rates.","Marks the end of the data.","BOOTSTRAP switches on resampling for the commands that follow.","Use simple random sampling with replacement.","Resample the variable hr.","95% percentile intervals from 5,000 resamples.","Leave out cases with user-missing values.","EXAMINE computes descriptive statistics for hr.","Turn off plots.","Ask for the descriptives table."],"read":"In the Descriptives output, the ordinary table shows a 95% Confidence Interval for Mean of about 61.9 to 74.1. The Bootstrap table shows the bootstrap Std. Error near 2.0 and a 95% interval near 64.4 to 72.0. Results are close to Python's."},"stata":{"code":"clear\ninput hr\n62\n70\n68\n75\n65\nend\nbootstrap mean = r(mean), reps(5000) seed(2025): summarize hr\nestat bootstrap, percentile\nci means hr","note":"estat bootstrap, percentile shows the bootstrap SE and the percentile interval; ci means gives the t-interval. Bootstrap results are close to Python's, not identical.","explain":["Clear any data in memory.","Start entering data for one variable hr.","Data row 1 of 5: a heart rate, typed on its own line so Stata reads it as a new case.","Data row 2 of 5: a heart rate, typed on its own line so Stata reads it as a new case.","Data row 3 of 5: a heart rate, typed on its own line so Stata reads it as a new case.","Data row 4 of 5: a heart rate, typed on its own line so Stata reads it as a new case.","Data row 5 of 5: a heart rate, typed on its own line so Stata reads it as a new case.","Stop entering data.","Resample 5,000 times (seeded) and record the mean each time.","Show the bootstrap standard error and the percentile interval.","Show the ordinary t-interval for comparison."],"read":"After estat bootstrap, look at the Std. err. (about 2.0) and the row labelled (P) for the percentile interval, about 64.4 to 72. The ci means table gives the t-interval, 61.85 to 74.15. Numbers are close to, not identical to, Python."},"conclusion":"The mean heart rate is 68.0 bpm. Resampling gives a standard error of about 2.0 (the formula gives 2.21) and a 95% interval of 64.4 to 72.0, against the t-interval 61.9 to 74.1. Caution: with only five readings the bootstrap interval is too narrow to trust; the wider t-interval is the safer statement.","practice":{"scenario":"A commuter logged the minutes of eight trips to work. Travel times are skewed by the odd bad day, so she wants the typical trip measured by the median, which has no simple standard error formula. Use the bootstrap.","data":"[24, 31, 28, 45, 26, 33, 29, 52]","tasks":["Find the sample median and mean.","Bootstrap the median with 5,000 resamples and report its standard error.","Report a 95% percentile interval for the median.","Compare with the bootstrap SE of the mean."],"hint":"Use rng.choice(commute, size=(5000, 8), replace=True), then np.median(..., axis=1) and np.percentile(..., [2.5, 97.5]).","solution":{"code":"import numpy as np\ncommute = np.array([24, 31, 28, 45, 26, 33, 29, 52])\nrng = np.random.default_rng(4)\nres = rng.choice(commute, size=(5000, len(commute)), replace=True)\nmeds = np.median(res, axis=1)\nmeans = res.mean(axis=1)\nprint('sample median =', np.median(commute), ' sample mean =', commute.mean())\nprint(f'bootstrap SE of median = {meds.std(ddof=1):.2f}')\nprint(f'bootstrap SE of mean   = {means.std(ddof=1):.2f}')\nprint('95% interval for the median:', np.percentile(meds, [2.5, 97.5]))","answer":"The sample median is 30.0 minutes and the mean is 33.5 (pulled up by the 45 and 52). The bootstrap SE of the median is 3.91, larger than the SE of the mean at 3.26. The 95% percentile interval for the median is 26 to 45 minutes. The median is less precise here, and its interval is wide, because only eight trips is very little data.","out":"sample median = 30.0  sample mean = 33.5\nbootstrap SE of median = 3.91\nbootstrap SE of mean   = 3.26\n95% interval for the median: [26. 45.]","figs":[]}}});
